:: Assessment
<div class="eyebrow">An opportunity is not the end of a story</div><h1>What your choices leave open</h1><<ownAssessment>><details><summary>Recall all source cards</summary><<ownSource>></details><details><summary>Questions you did not choose</summary><h3>I ask, “What does code-meshing ask a writer to try?”</h3><p>“Put the sentence on the desk,” the imagined Young replies. “Try keeping the home-language expression while giving unfamiliar readers a way into its meaning. What does that let you do?” See card B for the published proposal.</p><h3>I ask, “Does this mean conventions no longer matter?”</h3><p>“Show me the edit,” the imagined Young replies. “What does it help a reader understand? What does it change about the voice? Give those questions separate answers.” Read card D before treating code-meshing as the end of revision.</p><h3>I ask, “What work could a reader do?”</h3><p>“What did you do when you met Jordan?” the imagined Young asks. “I could infer, ask, look something up—or make a judgement before listening. Which action gives you evidence?” Return to card A.</p><h3>I ask, “What if the reader genuinely cannot understand a phrase?”</h3><p>“Then we have work to do,” the imagined Young replies. “Try context, an explanation, or a different example. Ask the reader again. Did the change solve the confusion you actually found?”</p><h3>I ask, “What if the evaluator still insists on a single standard?”</h3><p>“Whose risk are you choosing?” the imagined Young asks. “Lin has to live with Friday. Ask what that opportunity means before deciding what someone else should give up. Then explain what my advice does—and cannot do.”</p><h3>I ask, “Can I question the argument without dismissing it?”</h3><p>“Give the disagreement something to stand on,” the imagined Young replies. “State the limit, support it, then tell me what follows. I want an argument I can answer—not a smaller version of my claim that is easier to knock down.”</p><h3>I ask, “How could we explore this in our multilingual school in China?”</h3><p>“Start with examples people choose to share,” the imagined Young suggests. “Who is speaking to whom, for what purpose? Then ask where the comparison helps and where it breaks.” This application does not equate multilingual school experiences with the essay’s racial history.</p><h3>I ask, “What would a useful magazine revision actually change?”</h3><p>The imagined Young folds a page down the middle. “Here: what the edit helps readers understand. There: what it changes about the voice. Give Lin both columns, not just my conclusion.”</p><h3>I ask, “What should fair assessment reward?”</h3><p>“Try assessing the argument before admiring its suit,” the imagined Young replies. “Where is the evidence? What connects it to the claim? Who can follow it? Now ask what the language choices are doing.”</p></details><<button "Replay with a different approach">><<if window.parent === window>><<run setup.attemptId=crypto.randomUUID();setup.choices=[];setup.driverAttempts=[];setup.refresh()>><<else>><<run setup.send("replay")>><</if>><</button>><<button "Return to The Game">><<run setup.send("exit")>><</button>><details><summary>Sources and fiction</summary><p>Core text: Vershawn Ashanti Young, “Should Writers Use They Own English?”, Iowa Journal of Cultural Studies 12 (2010), 110–118. Full article linked on every source card. Read the entire assigned essay; cards are short excerpts and reading guides. Checked October 9, 2026. No generated answer represents Young’s personal view. The AI uses the essay cards and Young’s first-person faculty statement, not an invented corpus of other writings. Episode concepts: objection, concession, qualification, audience, code-meshing, evidence. AP W09 vocabulary in this episode: line of reasoning, sophistication, emphatic positioning, rhythm, coherent, evoke. Based on David’s weekly vocabulary schedule for October 12–16, 2026.</p></details>
